  {
   "claim": "Repetition increases perceived truth even when people know the answer (sari/kilt example)",
   "url": "https://psycnet.apa.org/manuscript/2015-38275-001.pdf",
   "note": "Fazio, Brashier, Payne & Marsh 2015, J. Experimental Psychology: General 144(5):993-1002. 'Reading a statement like A sari is the name of the short pleated skirt worn by Scots increased participants' later belief that it was true, even if they could correctly answer...'"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Single prior exposure increases perceived accuracy of fake news, persisting after a week; no effect for entirely implausible claims ('The Earth is a perfect square')",
   "url": "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=2958246",
   "note": "Pennycook, Cannon & Rand 2018, J. Experimental Psychology: General 147(12):1865-1880."
  }
 ],
 "caveats": "Narration compresses Pennycook's boundary condition: only entirely implausible claims were immune, and a small degree of plausibility sufficed. The effect is reliable but typically modest in size (a shift in ratings, not wholesale conversion). Fazio 2015 found knowledge doesn't fully protect; it doesn't mean knowledge never helps. Pennycook 2018: 'raised belief a week later' refers to perceived accuracy ratings persisting after a one-week delay. Round-3 visuals: the 1977 bars rising across sessions are schematic (ratings rose modestly on a 7-point scale), not the paper's means. Round-4 edit: quote marks removed from narration text. Round-5 edit: phonetic 'say' respellings removed (they hurt recognition). Line 3 now describes Fazio et al. 2015's example item without naming it in narration: the false statement was that a sari is the short pleated skirt worn by Scots (it is a kilt); the on-screen bubble shows it. 'Absurd' is now 'Ridiculous' (Pennycook 2018: only entirely implausible claims were immune). Round-6 edit: line 2 names the effect as 'illusory truth' (short for the illusory truth effect) so the term reads as one caption.",
